THE FIX. `dontAsk` is gone from ACT-S007's clause and the rule is version 2. Claude Code's settings reference says the mode "auto-denies every call that would otherwise prompt; reads, other actions that need no approval, and pre-approved tools still run", and the permission-modes page adds that it denies MORE than the default does: explicit `ask` rules, `AskUserQuestion` even where an allow rule matches it, connector tools an organization set to `ask`, and MCP tools marked `requiresUserInteraction`. Everything that runs without a prompt under `dontAsk` runs without a prompt under `default` too, so the mode removes no confirmation and grants nothing. A repository that commits it has narrowed itself, and Actaira was reporting it at the highest severity the pack has. The rule's sentence was rewritten in both catalogues to say what the clause now catches - "removes a confirmation the vendor's own default requires" - rather than "skips or automates the prompts", which `dontAsk` did satisfy on a reading; leaving the old text would have left the identifier meaning one thing in prose and another in its clause.

THE PRINCIPLE, now written down as the fourth prohibition in `packs/core/claude-code.toml`'s header, which `vendors.toml` inherits by its own "same contract" line. NO RULE FIRES ON A VALUE STRICTLY NARROWER THAN THE VENDOR'S OWN DEFAULT. Reporting a repository for locking itself down is a finding nobody can act on, and the remediation would be "loosen this". What makes it a defect and not an oversight is that the precedent was ALREADY IN THE TREE and was being contradicted: `resolve.py:1778` excludes Gemini's `plan` from `guardrail_removed` with the argument that "the reference calls it read-only mode, so it is more restrictive than the default rather than less, and a rule that fired on it would be reporting a repository for tightening its own configuration". Two readings of one principle, in one product, nine lines apart.

THE SWEEP, for whether any other rule does the same in another vendor. Command:
    python -c "import tomllib, pathlib
for p in sorted(pathlib.Path('src/actaira/surface/packs/core').glob('*.toml')):
    for r in tomllib.loads(p.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))['rule']:
        for c in r.get('when', []):
            print(r['id'], r['vendor'], c['fact'], c['op'], c.get('value', ''))"
Thirty-two rules, thirty-three clauses. Twenty-nine are `is_true` or `is_false` over a boolean the resolver minted, across seventeen distinct facts, and only one of those seventeen - `guardrail_removed` - is minted by choosing among a vendor's published alternatives. Its three minting sites set it true only for the WIDEST value each vendor publishes: `danger-full-access` of Codex's three sandbox modes, `never` of its four approval policies, `auto_edit` of Gemini's three. The other sixteen facts are structural - a path exists, a host is loopback, a launch names a version, a mount names a credential - and no vendor orders them, so "narrower than the default" does not apply. Four clauses compare against a written value list: ACT-S006 and ACT-S010 name KEY names, where presence is the finding and there is no narrower alternative to confuse it with; ACT-S009 names four spellings of unrestricted `Bash`, all of them widenings. ACT-S007's `mode` was the only clause in the tree naming a vendor's mode alternatives, and `dontAsk` was the only narrowing named anywhere. RESULT: ZERO OTHERS.

WHAT IT COST TO FIND. Not a failing test - nothing was red. It came out of reading the vendor's page for phase S3.1's inventory, which is the point: `dontAsk` is a value Anthropic publishes and this tree had never read the sentence that defines it.
